<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch2-page2-dominated-strategies" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Dominated Strategies</title>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-2-3">
    <title>Section 2.3, Dominated Strategies</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 2.3.</alert>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-2-5">
    <title>Section 2.5, Global Warming</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 2.5.</alert>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    We'll show that spending <m>0</m> strictly dominates any <m>x_1 &gt; 0</m>
    for Player 1 (the same argument works for any other country
    <m>i</m>). Set up the difference in payoffs, labeled:
  </p>

  <md>
    <mrow>
      \underbrace{\pi_1(0, x_2, \ldots, x_{10})}_{\text{Player 1's payoff when Player 1 spends } 0}
      -
      \underbrace{\pi_1(x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_{10})}_{\text{Player 1's payoff when Player 1 spends } x_1 &gt; 0}
    </mrow>
  </md>

  <p>
    which simplifies to:
  </p>

  <md>
    <mrow>
      = -\tfrac{1}{5}x_1 + x_1 = \tfrac{4}{5}x_1 \;&gt;\; 0 \quad \text{(simplified)}
    </mrow>
  </md>

  <p>
    Since this difference is positive, <m>x_1 = 0</m> strictly dominates
    any <m>x_1 &gt; 0</m> for Player 1, and the same argument applies to
    every other country.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-global-warming-pd">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Why does this global warming game count as a version of the
        Prisoner's Dilemma?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>Every country has a strictly dominant strategy (spend
          nothing), but if every country instead spent the "dominated"
          amount of <m>1</m>, every single country would end up better
          off.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Spending nothing gives every country a payoff of
            <m>0</m>, but if all ten countries spent <m>1</m>, every
            country's payoff would be <m>1</m> instead, strictly better.
            Rational individual behavior (everyone defects) leads to a
            worse outcome for everyone than if they'd all cooperated,
            exactly the Prisoner's Dilemma pattern, just with ten players
            instead of two.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Because each country's payoff depends on what the other
          countries do.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite, that's true of almost every game we've studied so
            far, not just Prisoner's Dilemma-type games. Something more
            specific has to hold.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Because there are more than two players.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite, the Prisoner's Dilemma pattern isn't about the
            number of players, the original version had only two.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-2-6">
    <title>Section 2.6, Hagar's Battles</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 2.6.</alert>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Before you finish the reading for Section 2.7, let's explore the first
    part of the strict dominance argument together, since the book's table
    can be hard to parse on a first read without having thought it through
    yourself first.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-2-7-part1">
    <title>Section 2.7, Second-Price Auctions (Part 1)</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 2.7, up through where the book states the payoff
      formula <m>\pi_i(b_1,\ldots,b_n)</m>.</alert>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Here's that formula again, labeled:
  </p>

  <md>
    <mrow>
      \underbrace{\pi_i(b_1,\ldots,b_n)}_{\text{payoff to player } i \text{ when the bids are } b_1,\ldots,b_n} \;=\;
      \begin{cases}
      \overbrace{0}^{\substack{\text{payoff is } 0 \text{ when player } i\text{'s bid is less than}\\ \text{the highest bid of the others (receives and pays nothing)}}} &amp; \text{if } b_i &lt; h_i, \\[2ex]
      \underbrace{v_i - h_i}_{\substack{\text{player } i\text{'s bid is higher than everyone else's:}\\ \text{payoff is what they value it minus what they actually pay}}} &amp; \text{if } h_i &lt; b_i.
      \end{cases}
    </mrow>
  </md>

  <p>
    One thing worth pinning down: in the second case, player <m>i</m> pays
    <m>h_i</m> &#8212; the highest bid <em>among the other players</em>
    &#8212; not their own bid <m>b_i</m>. That's the defining feature of a
    second-price auction: the winner pays what the runner-up bid, not
    their own bid.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now, using just that formula, work out for yourself why bidding your
    true value <m>v_1</m> weakly dominates every other bid <m>b_1</m>. The
    two checkpoints below cover the two cases.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-auction-v-less-h">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>v_1 &lt; h_1</m> (the object is worth less to you than
        the highest bid among the others &#8212; you shouldn't win). Why
        does bidding <m>v_1</m> weakly dominate every other bid in this
        case?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>Bidding <m>v_1</m> gives payoff <m>0</m>. Bidding lower also
          gives <m>0</m> (you still lose, nothing changes). But bidding
          high enough to cross above <m>h_1</m> makes you win, paying more
          than the object is worth to you, a negative payoff. So <m>v_1</m>
          is never worse than another bid, and sometimes strictly
          better.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Correct. Since you shouldn't win this auction, the only way
          to do worse than <m>v_1</m> is to accidentally win it by
          overbidding.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Bidding higher than <m>v_1</m> is always safe, since you
          only pay <m>h_1</m> regardless of your own bid.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Not quite &#8212; that's true when you were already going to
          win. Here <m>v_1 &lt; h_1</m>, so bidding high enough to win
          means paying <m>h_1</m>, which is more than the object is worth
          to you.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Bidding <m>v_1</m> always wins the auction, so it's the
          obvious best choice.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Not quite &#8212; here <m>v_1 &lt; h_1</m>, so bidding
          <m>v_1</m> loses the auction. The dominance argument doesn't
          depend on winning.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-auction-h-less-v">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Now suppose <m>h_1 &lt; v_1</m> (the object is worth more to you
        than the highest bid among the others &#8212; you should win). Why
        does bidding <m>v_1</m> weakly dominate every other bid in this
        case?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>Bidding <m>v_1</m> gives payoff <m>v_1 - h_1 &gt; 0</m>.
          Bidding higher still wins and still pays <m>h_1</m>, so the
          payoff is unchanged. Bidding lower, but still above <m>h_1</m>,
          also still wins with the same payoff. But bidding low enough to
          drop below <m>h_1</m> makes you lose entirely, dropping your
          payoff to <m>0</m> &#8212; worse. So <m>v_1</m> is never worse
          than another bid, and sometimes strictly better.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Correct. Since you were already going to win by bidding
          <m>v_1</m>, overbidding can't help &#8212; you still just pay
          <m>h_1</m>. The only danger is underbidding far enough to lose
          on purpose.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Bidding higher than <m>v_1</m> increases your payoff, since
          you'll win by a wider margin.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Not quite &#8212; the price you pay is <m>h_1</m>, the
          runner-up's bid, not your own. Bidding higher than <m>v_1</m>
          doesn't change what you pay, so your payoff stays exactly
          <m>v_1 - h_1</m>.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Bidding lower than <m>v_1</m> is always fine, since you'll
          still win as long as you bid something positive.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Not quite &#8212; if you bid low enough to drop below
          <m>h_1</m>, you lose the auction entirely, and your payoff drops
          from <m>v_1-h_1</m> to <m>0</m>.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-2-7-part2">
    <title>Section 2.7, Second-Price Auctions (Part 2)</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read the rest of Section 2.7.</alert>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    If the reasoning in the checkpoints made sense to you, you've already
    done the hard part of what the book's table is showing &#8212; just in
    words instead of rows. The table can be genuinely hard to parse on a
    first read without having thought it through yourself first, which is
    exactly why we did the checkpoints before you got here.
  </p>

  <p>
    Those checkpoints together prove the first condition needed to show
    <m>v_1</m> weakly dominates any other bid <m>b_1</m>: for every
    possible combination of the other players' bids,
  </p>

  <md>
    <mrow>\pi_1(v_1,b_2,\ldots,b_n) \;\geq\; \pi_1(b_1,b_2,\ldots,b_n).</mrow>
  </md>

  <p>
    The book then shows the second condition needed for weak dominance:
    that there's <em>at least one</em> combination of the other players'
    bids where the inequality is strict,
  </p>

  <md>
    <mrow>\pi_1(v_1,b_2,\ldots,b_n) \;&gt;\; \pi_1(b_1,b_2,\ldots,b_n).</mrow>
  </md>

  <p>
    Here's a quick summary of that argument, without all the detail,
    since you can read the full version in the book or think it through
    yourself. Take any <m>b_1 \ne v_1</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    <term>Case 1: <m>b_1 &lt; v_1</m></term>. Suppose the other players'
    bids are such that <m>h_1 = \max(b_2,\ldots,b_n)</m> satisfies
    <m>b_1 &lt; h_1 &lt; v_1</m>. Then
  </p>

  <md>
    <mrow>
      \underbrace{\pi_1(v_1,b_2,\ldots,b_n)}_{\substack{v_1 &gt; h_1\text{, so you win.}\\ \text{Payoff: } v_1 - h_1 &gt; 0}}
      \;&gt;\;
      \underbrace{\pi_1(b_1,b_2,\ldots,b_n)}_{\substack{b_1 &lt; h_1\text{, so you lose.}\\ \text{Payoff: } 0}}
    </mrow>
  </md>

  <p>
    Why? Think it through yourself before checking the box below.
  </p>

  <solution>
    <title>Justification</title>
    <p>
      On the left, bidding <m>v_1</m> wins the auction, since
      <m>v_1 &gt; h_1</m>, so the payoff is <m>v_1 - h_1 &gt; 0</m>. On
      the right, bidding <m>b_1</m> loses, since <m>b_1 &lt; h_1</m>, so
      the payoff is <m>0</m>. Bidding your true value wins you an auction
      that underbidding would have cost you.
    </p>
  </solution>

  <p>
    <term>Case 2: <m>b_1 &gt; v_1</m></term>. Now suppose instead
    <m>h_1</m> satisfies <m>v_1 &lt; h_1 &lt; b_1</m>. Then
  </p>

  <md>
    <mrow>
      \underbrace{\pi_1(v_1,b_2,\ldots,b_n)}_{\substack{v_1 &lt; h_1\text{, so you lose.}\\ \text{Payoff: } 0}}
      \;&gt;\;
      \underbrace{\pi_1(b_1,b_2,\ldots,b_n)}_{\substack{b_1 &gt; h_1\text{, so you win, but pay}\\ h_1 &gt; v_1.\text{ Payoff: } v_1-h_1 &lt; 0}}
    </mrow>
  </md>

  <p>
    Why? Again, try it yourself first.
  </p>

  <solution>
    <title>Justification</title>
    <p>
      On the left, bidding <m>v_1</m> loses, since <m>v_1 &lt; h_1</m>,
      so the payoff is <m>0</m>. On the right, bidding <m>b_1</m> wins,
      since <m>b_1 &gt; h_1</m>, but you're paying <m>h_1</m> for
      something only worth <m>v_1 &lt; h_1</m> to you, so the payoff is
      <m>v_1 - h_1 &lt; 0</m>. Overbidding here wins you an auction you'd
      have been better off losing.
    </p>
  </solution>

</section>
